  [1mThe fork:[0m whether every call site passes identity explicitly, or the tracking module holds the current user and
  attaches it automatically. This sets the module's public interface, so it's the expensive one to change — it's the
  signature every future form will be written against.

  - [1mExplicit per call[0m — [38;5;153mtrack('login', { userId })[39m everywhere. Buys a stateless module that's trivial to test and has no
    hidden dependencies. Costs: every future form has to obtain and plumb a [38;5;153muserId[39m it may not have on hand, which is
    the same plumbing problem that made the original parameter awkward.
  - [1mModule holds current user[0m — [38;5;153midentify(userId)[39m once on successful login, then [38;5;153mtrack('form_submit', {...})[39m from
    anywhere and identity is attached. Buys call sites that don't think about identity at all. Costs module-scoped
    mutable state, which needs a clear single writer and an explicit clear-on-logout, and makes tests order-dependent
    unless there's a reset.
  - [1mRead from session storage[0m — tracking pulls identity from wherever session state already lives. Buys no new state.
    Costs a dependency on a session layer this repo doesn't have yet — so it's really "build sessions first."

  [1mMy recommendation: module holds current user.[0m It's the standard analytics shape ([38;5;153midentify[39m then [38;5;153mtrack[39m) and it's the one
  that makes your stated goal — other forms adopting this later — actually cheap. The mutable-state cost is real but
  contained: login is the only writer, and the spec will require an explicit reset for logout and tests.

  One wrinkle either way, which I'll write into the spec rather than ask about: a [1mfailed[0m login has no [38;5;153muserId[39m —
  authentication is what produces it. So failed attempts can only be attributed to the attempted username, and the event
  schema needs identity to be optional. That's worth tracking (failed logins are the interesting audit signal), but it
  means "track who logged in" has two distinct event shapes, not one.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Identity [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mHow does the tracking module learn who the current user is?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153midentify() then track() (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mLogin calls identify(userId) once; later events attach identity automatically. Cheapest for the future forms you [39m
     [38;5;246mmentioned. Needs a single writer and an explicit reset.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Explicit on every call
     [38;5;246mEach call passes userId itself. Stateless and easy to test, but every future form must obtain and plumb the ID.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Read from a session layer
     [38;5;246mTracking reads identity from session state. No new state, but this repo has no session layer — it means building [39m
     [38;5;246msessions first, widening the scope.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
